Chapter: X86-64 Code Generator

 The x86-64 instruction selector for the flat LIR (the ratchet step of
 the LIR design). This is the
 first codegen-changing step of the middle-end campaign -- where the LIR the
 `IR/Lir.codex` chapter lowers and allocates finally becomes machine code
 rather than an opt-in dump. It runs behind a per-function dispatch: a
 function whose body lies entirely within a construct whitelist is lowered
 tree -> LIR -> allocated -> x86 here, and everything else falls back to the
 existing recursive tree emitter (`emit-function-standard` /
 `emit-function-minimal`), byte-for-byte unchanged.

 **This increment covers straight-line arithmetic, branches, spills,
 constructor-pattern matches, and direct calls:** integer literals,
 parameters, `let`, add/subtract/multiply, `if` / `match` (literal, variable,
 wildcard, or, and constructor patterns), spills, and calls to plain user
 functions (`LiCall`) -- placing arguments in the argument registers, keeping
 the heap-collision stack guard for a non-leaf, and leaving self-tail-
 recursive functions on the tree path where TCO turns them into loops. A
 value live across a call is safe because the allocator only uses callee-saved
 registers, which the callee preserves. A spilled operand is loaded from its slot into a
 scratch before use; a spilled destination is computed in a scratch and
 stored back; a spilled parameter is stored from its argument register into
 its slot at entry. A constructor pattern loads the variant tag and each
 bound field from the scrutinee pointer (`LiLoad`). The selector now handles
 every LIR instruction the lowering emits, so what falls back is exactly what
 the lowering itself declines: calls, vector patterns, real guards, act
 blocks, closures, and everything else -- plus, here, functions with more
 than six parameters (a seventh arrives on the caller's stack, not in a
 register). The whitelist grows one construct per CL.

 The register file is the concrete form of "the register file is data": the
 allocator's abstract registers `0..4` map through `lir-phys` to the four
 callee-saved locals `RBX, R12, R13, R14` at `0..3` (the same four the tree
 emitter fills first) and the caller-saved `RAX` at `4`. RAX is handed out
 only to a value not live across a call -- the allocator's crossing analysis
 restricts a cross-call value to the callee-saved pool -- so nothing kept in
 RAX is clobbered by the call the callee makes. The reserved roles the design
 names -- R10 the bump allocator, R15 the closure env, RBP, RSP -- never
 arise; the whitelist has no closures and no frame pointer. Two more
 caller-saved registers the allocator never hands out, R11 and R8, are the
 scratch: R11 holds a spilled destination or an immediate compared on the
 left, R8 a spilled or oversized second operand, both consumed within a
 single instruction's selection and never live across a call. Spill slots
 live at `[RSP + slot*8]` under a fixed-size frame the prologue reserves and
 the epilogue releases.

Section: LIR Register File (x86-64)

 An abstract allocator register `k` (a `LocReg k`) is the physical register
 `lir-phys[k]`: the four callee-saved locals `RBX, R12, R13, R14` at `0..3`,
 the caller-saved `RAX` at `4`, and the four argument registers `RDI, RSI,
 RDX, RCX` at `5..8`. A spill slot `k` (a `LocSlot k`) is the stack cell
 `[RSP + k*8]`.

 Everything from index 4 up is caller-saved, and the split at 4 is the whole
 register-file policy. A value live across a call cannot sit above it (a call
 clobbers all of it), so the allocator's crossing analysis confines crossers
 to `[0, 4)`. A function that makes a call never uses `5..8` at all -- it
 prefers RAX, then the callee pool -- which keeps the argument registers free
 for argument placement and means this extension changes nothing about how a
 non-leaf is compiled.

 A leaf is where it pays. A function that makes no call has nothing beneath it
 to clobber a caller-saved register, so it allocates `5..8` first and pushes
 nothing: `lir-nused-callee` counts only indices below 4 and finds none. And
 because `5..8` are the argument registers *in argument order*, and the
 parameters are scanned first against a free file, parameter `i` is handed
 `lir-phys[5+i]` -- the register it already arrived in -- so the entry moves
 become `mov rdi, rdi` and are elided. A leaf that needs more than five
 registers still falls back to the callee pool and pays for what it uses.

 R8/R9 are excluded deliberately: they are the selector's scratch (spilled
 operand staging and base pointers), as is R11.

  lir-phys : List Integer = [3, 12, 13, 14, reg-rax, 7, 6, 2, 1]

 The pool structure that indexes the mapping above, and it sits here rather
 than in `IR/Lir.codex` for one reason: `nregs` is the length of `lir-phys`,
 and two files cannot be trusted to keep that true. Split apart, an added
 register is an edit in one file and a silent out-of-range `list-at` in the
 other. Adjacent, the two are read together or not at all.

 `ncallee` is 4: `RBX, R12, R13, R14` are callee-saved and a value live across
 a call is confined to them. `nargregs` is 5, so `[5, 9)` is `RDI, RSI, RDX,
 RCX` in argument order and index 4 is `RAX` alone -- the register a
 non-crosser prefers and the one a leaf skips. `diva`/`divb` are 4 and 7
 because `idiv` takes the dividend in RDX:RAX and leaves the remainder in RDX,
 which are `lir-phys` indices 4 and 7; a target whose division fixes nothing
 writes -1 for both. `budget` is 12, comfortably above the 9 allocatable
 registers, because it sizes the verifier's occupant array and slack there is
 cheaper than an overflow.

  lir-target-x86 : LirTarget = LirTarget {
   ncallee = 4,
   nargregs = 5,
   nregs = 9,
   diva = 4,
   divb = 7,
   budget = 12
  }

  lir-phys-of : Location -> Integer
  lir-phys-of (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (r) -> list-at lir-phys r
    is LocSlot (s) -> 0 - 1

  lir-slot-off : Integer -> Integer
  lir-slot-off (k) = k * 8

 The number of callee-saved registers this function must save and restore --
 one past the highest `LocReg` index that is a callee-saved register (an
 abstract index below `ncallee`). RAX, the caller-saved fifth
 register (`lir-phys` index 4), is deliberately excluded: the callee may
 clobber it freely, so it is never pushed. Because the allocator hands out
 the callee pool lowest-free, the used callee indices are a prefix, so
 pushing `[0, this)` pushes exactly the ones in use. The slot count is one
 past the highest `LocSlot` index (the allocator gives each spilled vreg its
 own dedicated slot), and sizes the frame.

  lir-nused-callee : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-nused-callee (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at assign i)
    is LocReg (r) -> lir-nused-callee assign (i + 1) n (if (r < (lir-target-x86.ncallee)) & ((r + 1) > acc) then r + 1 else acc)
    is LocSlot (s) -> lir-nused-callee assign (i + 1) n acc

  lir-nslots : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-nslots (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at assign i)
    is LocSlot (s) -> lir-nslots assign (i + 1) n (if (s + 1) > acc then s + 1 else acc)
    is LocReg (r) -> lir-nslots assign (i + 1) n acc

  lir-scratch-reg : Integer = reg-r11

  lir-scratch2 : Integer = reg-r8

  imm-fits-i32 : Integer -> Boolean
  imm-fits-i32 (k) = (k >= (0 - 2147483648)) & (k <= 2147483647)

Section: LIR Instruction Selection (x86-64)

 The allocator guarantees the destination location of an instruction never
 coincides with a live operand's location: a def's interval begins where the
 operands are still live, the strict-expire sweep keeps their registers
 occupied, and each spilled vreg has its own dedicated slot -- so a def and
 its operands are always distinct registers or distinct slots. That is what
 lets selection compute a result in one working register and place it,
 without an aliasing case to guard.

 Load an operand into a specific register `w`: an immediate via `li` (which
 already picks xor / mov-imm32 / mov-imm64 by magnitude), a register operand
 via `mov-rr`, a spilled operand via a load from its stack slot.

  lir-load-into : CodegenState, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-load-into (st) (w) (v) (assign) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> st-append-code st (li w k)
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> st-append-code st (mov-rr w (list-at lir-phys pr))
      is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code st (mov-load w reg-rsp (lir-slot-off ks))

 Apply add/subtract/multiply of a right operand into the working register,
 which already holds the left operand. A register right operand uses the two-
 register form; a spilled right operand is loaded into R8 first; an immediate
 that fits a signed 32-bit field uses the immediate form (multiply by
 immediate is the three-operand `imul-rri`), and a wider immediate is staged
 into R8 -- so no arithmetic instruction ever truncates a literal.

  lir-alu-rr : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-alu-rr (st) (op) (pd) (pr) =
   when op
    is LoAdd -> st-append-code st (add-rr pd pr)
    is LoSub -> st-append-code st (sub-rr pd pr)
    is LoMul -> st-append-code st (imul-rr pd pr)
    is LoRem -> st
    is LoDiv -> st

 A remainder never reaches these: `lir-sel-bin` routes `LoRem` to `idiv`
 before the two-operand ALU is consulted, because a remainder is not one. The
 arms are here because the match must be exhaustive, and they do nothing
 rather than encode an instruction that does not exist.

  lir-alu-imm : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-alu-imm (st) (op) (pd) (k) =
   when op
    is LoAdd -> st-append-code st (add-ri pd k)
    is LoSub -> st-append-code st (sub-ri pd k)
    is LoMul -> st-append-code st (imul-rri pd pd k)
    is LoRem -> st
    is LoDiv -> st

  lir-alu-gen : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-alu-gen (st) (op) (w) (rhs) (assign) =
   when rhs
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> lir-alu-rr st op w (list-at lir-phys pr)
      is LocSlot (ks) -> lir-alu-rr (st-append-code st (mov-load lir-scratch2 reg-rsp (lir-slot-off ks))) op w lir-scratch2
    is LvImm (k) ->
     if imm-fits-i32 k then lir-alu-imm st op w k
     else lir-alu-rr (st-append-code st (li lir-scratch2 k)) op w lir-scratch2

 A binary op: compute in a working register -- the destination register when
 the def has one, else R11 -- by loading the left operand and applying the
 operator with the right, then place the working register into the def's slot
 if it was spilled.

 Fusing an add or subtract of a constant into an `lea`. x86's ALU is
 two-operand -- `dst op= src` -- so a binary whose destination is not its left
 operand has to move first: `v = n - 1` with `v` and `n` in different
 registers costs `mov v, n; sub v, 1`. `lea` reads a base and a displacement
 and writes a third register, so the same value is one instruction:
 `lea v, [n - 1]`.

 Only for add and subtract (a multiply is not a displacement), only for an
 immediate right operand that fits one, and only when the left operand is in
 a register that is *not* the destination. If it were the destination the move
 is already nothing -- `mov-rr` elides a self-move -- and `sub dst, 1` is
 optimal on its own; fusing there would trade one instruction for one
 instruction and change the bytes for no reason.

 `lea` does not write flags, where `add`/`sub` do. That is a safe direction
 and not a coincidence to rely on: the LIR never reuses arithmetic flags, it
 emits `LiCmp` to set them and `LiBranch` to read them, and the lowering only
 ever puts those two next to each other at the end of a block.

  lir-lea-src : LirValue, List Location -> Integer
  lir-lea-src (l) (assign) =
   when l
    is LvReg (lr) ->
     when (list-at assign lr)
      is LocReg (lpr) -> list-at lir-phys lpr
      is LocSlot (ks) -> 0 - 1
    is LvImm (k) -> 0 - 1

  lir-lea-disp : LirOp, LirValue -> Integer
  lir-lea-disp (op) (r) =
   when r
    is LvImm (k) -> when op is LoSub -> 0 - k is otherwise -> k
    is LvReg (rr) -> 0

  lir-lea-ok : LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> Boolean
  lir-lea-ok (op) (w) (l) (r) (assign) =
   when op
    is LoMul -> False
    is otherwise ->
     when r
      is LvReg (rr) -> False
      is LvImm (k) ->
       if imm-fits-i32 (lir-lea-disp op r) == False then False
       else let lw = lir-lea-src l assign
       in if lw < 0 then False
          else lw /= w

 The same trade for a sum of two registers. `v = a + b` with all three in
 different registers is `mov v, a; add v, b`; `lea v, [a + b]` is one
 instruction. Add only -- there is no `lea` for a difference of two registers,
 and a product only for the scale factors.

 Both operands must be in registers, and neither may be the destination. The
 allocator does not hand a definition the register of a live operand, so a
 binary over three distinct vregs always qualifies; the exclusions are for the
 shapes where the destination *is* an operand, which is how a loop's own
 parameter update looks (`v1 = add v1 v0`). There the move is already elided
 and `add v1, v0` is one instruction, so fusing would trade nothing for
 nothing and change the bytes.

  lir-lea-rr-ok : LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> Boolean
  lir-lea-rr-ok (op) (w) (l) (r) (assign) =
   when op
    is LoAdd ->
     let lw = lir-lea-src l assign
     in let rw = lir-lea-src r assign
     in if lw < 0 then False
        else if rw < 0 then False
        else if lw == w then False
        else rw /= w
    is otherwise -> False

 The destination sharing the right operand's register. The two-operand
 lowering is `mov w, l; op w, r`, which writes `w` before it reads `r`, so
 that sequence would read a value it had just destroyed. It became reachable
 when the numbering started modelling read-then-write and let a definition
 take a register its operand was still nominally holding.

 `w` already holds `r`, which is most of the work. Add and multiply commute,
 so `op w, l` is the answer and it is one instruction rather than two.
 Subtract does not: `l - r` with `r` in `w` is `-(r - l)`, so negate and add.
 Two instructions -- the same as the move-and-subtract it replaces, and
 correct, which that would not be.

  lir-alias-r : Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> Boolean
  lir-alias-r (w) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let rw = lir-lea-src r assign
   in if rw /= w then False
      else (lir-lea-src l assign) /= w

  lir-sel-bin-aliased : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-bin-aliased (st) (op) (w) (l) (assign) =
   when op
    is LoSub -> lir-alu-gen (st-append-code st (neg-r w)) LoAdd w l assign
    is otherwise -> lir-alu-gen st op w l assign

 A remainder, and the only operator here that does not get to pick its
 registers. `idiv` divides RDX:RAX by its operand and leaves the remainder in
 RDX, so the dividend is moved into RAX, `cqo` sign-extends it across RDX, and
 the remainder is taken from RDX afterwards. The allocator has already kept
 every value that is live across this instruction out of both registers, so
 moving into them here destroys nothing.

 The divisor is the one thing that needs care. `idiv` reads it *after*
 RDX:RAX is set up, so it cannot be sitting in either of them -- and it cannot
 be an immediate, which `idiv` has no form for, nor a spill slot. In any of
 those cases it goes through the scratch register first. R11 is never
 allocated, so nothing is displaced by borrowing it.

 The two moves that bracket this both tend to vanish. The remainder's vreg is
 hinted to RDX, so taking it out is `mov rdx, rdx`; and where the dividend is
 already in RAX, putting it there is `mov rax, rax`.

 Dividing or taking a remainder by a positive power of two is a shift or a
 mask. The tree emitter has always done this -- `collatz` is `test rdi, 1` and
 `sar rdi, 1` there, not two `idiv` sequences -- and the LIR emits the same
 instructions from the same rule, because two emitters that disagree about
 what a program means is a worse problem than a slow one.

  lir-sel-pow2 : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, Integer, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-pow2 (st) (op) (w) (l) (k) (assign) =
   let st1 = lir-load-into st w l assign
   in when op
    is LoDiv -> st-append-code st1 (sar-ri w (int-log2 k))
    is otherwise -> st-append-code st1 (and-ri w (k - 1))

  lir-imm-val : LirValue -> Integer
  lir-imm-val (v) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> k
    is LvReg (r) -> 0

 The general form. `idiv` divides RDX:RAX by its operand, leaving the quotient
 in RAX and the remainder in RDX, so the dividend is moved into RAX, `cqo`
 sign-extends it across RDX, and the answer is taken from whichever register
 holds it. The allocator has already kept every value live across this
 instruction out of both, so moving into them destroys nothing, and the
 result's vreg is hinted at the register it will land in, so taking it out
 usually elides.

 The divisor is the one thing needing care: `idiv` reads it after RDX:RAX is
 set up, so it cannot be in either of them -- and it cannot be an immediate,
 which `idiv` has no form for, nor a spill slot. In any of those it goes
 through the scratch register first, which is never allocated and so displaces
 nothing.

  lir-sel-divrem : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-divrem (st) (op) (dst) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let rp = lir-lea-src r assign
   in let stage = if rp < 0 then True else if rp == reg-rax then True else rp == reg-rdx
   in let st1 = if stage then lir-load-into st lir-scratch-reg r assign else st
   in let dv = if stage then lir-scratch-reg else rp
   in let st2 = lir-load-into st1 reg-rax l assign
   in let st3 = st-append-code st2 cqo
   in let st4 = st-append-code st3 (idiv-r dv)
   in let res = when op is LoDiv -> reg-rax is otherwise -> reg-rdx
   in when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> lir-move-param-reg st4 (list-at lir-phys pr) res
    is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code st4 (mov-store reg-rsp res (lir-slot-off ks))

  lir-sel-rem : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-rem (st) (op) (dst) (l) (r) (assign) =
   if lir-imm-pow2 r == False then lir-sel-divrem st op dst l r assign
   else when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> lir-sel-pow2 st op (list-at lir-phys pr) l (lir-imm-val r) assign
    is LocSlot (ks) ->
     st-append-code (lir-sel-pow2 st op lir-scratch-reg l (lir-imm-val r) assign) (mov-store reg-rsp lir-scratch-reg (lir-slot-off ks))

 A multiply by a constant is one instruction. x86 has a three-operand `imul`
 that reads a register and an immediate and writes a third register, so
 loading the constant and multiplying in place -- `mov rax, 3; imul rax, rdi`
 -- is one instruction more than it needs to be. Multiplication commutes, so
 the constant may be on either side.

  lir-is-imm : LirValue -> Boolean
  lir-is-imm (v) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> True
    is LvReg (r) -> False

  lir-mul-rri-ok : LirOp, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> Boolean
  lir-mul-rri-ok (op) (l) (r) (assign) =
   when op
    is LoMul ->
     if lir-is-imm r then if (lir-lea-src l assign) >= 0 then imm-fits-i32 (lir-imm-val r) else False
     else if lir-is-imm l then if (lir-lea-src r assign) >= 0 then imm-fits-i32 (lir-imm-val l) else False
     else False
    is otherwise -> False

  lir-sel-mul-rri : CodegenState, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-mul-rri (st) (w) (l) (r) (assign) =
   if lir-is-imm r then st-append-code st (imul-rri w (lir-lea-src l assign) (lir-imm-val r))
   else st-append-code st (imul-rri w (lir-lea-src r assign) (lir-imm-val l))

  lir-sel-bin : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-bin (st) (op) (dst) (l) (r) (assign) =
   if lir-op-divides op then lir-sel-rem st op dst l r assign
   else lir-sel-alu st op dst l r assign

  lir-sel-alu : CodegenState, LirOp, Integer, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-alu (st) (op) (dst) (l) (r) (assign) =
   when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) ->
     let w = list-at lir-phys pr
     in if lir-lea-ok op w l r assign
        then st-append-code st (lea w (lir-lea-src l assign) (lir-lea-disp op r))
        else if lir-lea-rr-ok op w l r assign
        then st-append-code st (lea-rr w (lir-lea-src l assign) (lir-lea-src r assign))
        else if lir-mul-rri-ok op l r assign
        then lir-sel-mul-rri st w l r assign
        else if lir-alias-r w l r assign
        then lir-sel-bin-aliased st op w l assign
        else lir-alu-gen (lir-load-into st w l assign) op w r assign
    is LocSlot (ks) ->
     let w = lir-scratch-reg
     in st-append-code (lir-alu-gen (lir-load-into st w l assign) op w r assign) (mov-store reg-rsp w (lir-slot-off ks))

 A move: load the source into the destination register, or into R11 and
 store to the destination slot.

  lir-sel-mov : CodegenState, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-mov (st) (dst) (src) (assign) =
   when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> lir-load-into st (list-at lir-phys pr) src assign
    is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code (lir-load-into st lir-scratch-reg src assign) (mov-store reg-rsp lir-scratch-reg (lir-slot-off ks))

 A memory load reads `width` bytes from `[base + off]` into a destination
 register, sign- or zero-extending a narrow field to 64 bits. A full 8-byte
 load fills the register and needs no extension; 4/2/1-byte loads pick the
 signed or unsigned widening move. This is the constructor-pattern primitive
 -- the variant tag at offset 0 and each bound field at its packed offset.

  lir-emit-load-w : CodegenState, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-load-w (st) (dr) (breg) (off) (w) (sg) =
   if w >= 8 then st-append-code st (mov-load dr breg off)
   else if w == 4 then st-append-code st (if sg then movsxd dr breg off else mov-load-u32 dr breg off)
   else if w == 2 then st-append-code st (if sg then movsx-word dr breg off else movzx-word dr breg off)
   else st-append-code st (if sg then movsx-byte dr breg off else movzx-byte dr breg off)

  lir-sel-load : CodegenState, Integer, LirValue, Integer, Integer, Boolean, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-load (st) (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) (assign) =
   let bb = lir-load-oper st base lir-scratch2 assign
   in when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> lir-emit-load-w (bb.state) (list-at lir-phys pr) (bb.reg) off w sg
    is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code (lir-emit-load-w (bb.state) lir-scratch-reg (bb.reg) off w sg) (mov-store reg-rsp lir-scratch-reg (lir-slot-off ks))

 A call places each argument into its argument register (`arg-regs[i]`),
 emits the call (recording the call-site patch, resolved to the target's
 offset at finalize), and moves the RAX result into the destination. There is
 no argument-shuffle hazard: every argument value is an immediate, a value in
 a callee-saved register, or a spill slot -- never an argument register,
 because the allocator only ever uses callee-saved registers and parameters
 were moved out of the argument registers at entry. So the placements are
 independent and their order does not matter. Values live across the call
 survive it for the same reason: callee-saved registers are preserved by the
 callee, and spill slots are memory. At most six arguments (the lowering
 declines a seventh, which would need a stack push).

 Placing a call's arguments. Each moves into the register its position
 requires, and while the allocator kept every value out of the argument
 registers those moves were independent and could be emitted in order. The
 hints put values there deliberately -- that is the point, an argument already
 sitting where it belongs needs no move at all -- and the moves stop being
 independent the moment they do. `f b a` wants RDI <- RSI and RSI <- RDI, and
 whichever goes first destroys the other.

 So the same discipline the back-edge uses: emit a move only when nothing
 still pending reads the register it is about to write. That orders every
 acyclic shape, and with hints almost every shape is acyclic -- most arguments
 are already home and their move elides. A cycle has no such move by
 construction; stage one source into the scratch register, which is not an
 argument register and therefore nobody's destination, and the cycle becomes a
 chain. R11 is never allocated, so a source register equal to it means staged
 and nothing else.

 An argument coming from a slot or an immediate reads no register at all. It
 can never be the thing another move is waiting on, and it is never what a
 cycle is made of.

  lir-argsrcs : List LirValue, Integer, Integer, List Location, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-argsrcs (argvals) (i) (na) (assign) (acc) =
   if i >= na then acc
   else lir-argsrcs argvals (i + 1) na assign (list-push acc (lir-lea-src (list-at argvals i) assign))

  lir-argmv-blocked : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-argmv-blocked (srcs) (done) (i) (na) (dsti) (j) =
   if j >= na then False
   else if j == i then lir-argmv-blocked srcs done i na dsti (j + 1)
   else if list-at done j then lir-argmv-blocked srcs done i na dsti (j + 1)
   else if (list-at srcs j) == dsti then True
   else lir-argmv-blocked srcs done i na dsti (j + 1)

  lir-argmv-pick : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-argmv-pick (srcs) (done) (i) (na) =
   if i >= na then 0 - 1
   else if list-at done i then lir-argmv-pick srcs done (i + 1) na
   else if lir-argmv-blocked srcs done i na (list-at arg-regs i) 0 then lir-argmv-pick srcs done (i + 1) na
   else i

  lir-argmv-stageable : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-argmv-stageable (srcs) (done) (i) (na) =
   if i >= na then 0 - 1
   else if list-at done i then lir-argmv-stageable srcs done (i + 1) na
   else if (list-at srcs i) < 0 then lir-argmv-stageable srcs done (i + 1) na
   else if (list-at srcs i) == lir-scratch-reg then lir-argmv-stageable srcs done (i + 1) na
   else i

  lir-place-one : CodegenState, Integer, Integer, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-place-one (st) (dst) (srcreg) (val) (assign) =
   if srcreg == lir-scratch-reg then st-append-code st (mov-rr dst lir-scratch-reg)
   else when val
    is LvImm (k) -> st-append-code st (li dst k)
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> st-append-code st (mov-rr dst (list-at lir-phys pr))
      is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code st (mov-load dst reg-rsp (lir-slot-off ks))

  lir-place-loop : CodegenState, List LirValue, List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, List Location, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-place-loop (st) (argvals) (srcs) (done) (na) (assign) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then st
   else let p = lir-argmv-pick srcs done 0 na
   in if p >= 0
      then lir-place-loop (lir-place-one st (list-at arg-regs p) (list-at srcs p) (list-at argvals p) assign) argvals srcs (list-set-at done p True) na assign (fuel - 1)
      else let c = lir-argmv-stageable srcs done 0 na
      in if c < 0 then st
         else lir-place-loop (st-append-code st (mov-rr lir-scratch-reg (list-at srcs c))) argvals (list-set-at srcs c lir-scratch-reg) done na assign (fuel - 1)

  lir-place-args : CodegenState, List LirValue, Integer, Integer, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-place-args (st) (argvals) (i) (na) (assign) =
   lir-place-loop st argvals (lir-argsrcs argvals 0 na assign []) (lir-make-vec 0 na []) na assign (na * 2 + 4)

  lir-sel-call : CodegenState, Integer, Text, List LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-sel-call (st) (dst) (name) (argvals) (assign) =
   let st1 = lir-place-args st argvals 0 (list-length argvals) assign
   in let st2 = emit-call-to st1 name
   in when (list-at assign dst)
    is LocReg (pr) -> st-append-code st2 (mov-rr (list-at lir-phys pr) reg-rax)
    is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code st2 (mov-store reg-rsp reg-rax (lir-slot-off ks))

Section: LIR Compares and Branch Conditions

 A LIR condition maps to an x86 signed condition code -- Codex integers are
 signed 64-bit, so the signed forms (`l`, `g`, `le`, `ge`) are the correct
 ones.

  lir-cc-of : LirCond -> Integer
  lir-cc-of (c) =
   when c
    is LcEq -> cc-e
    is LcNe -> cc-ne
    is LcLt -> cc-l
    is LcGt -> cc-g
    is LcLe -> cc-le
    is LcGe -> cc-ge

 An operand in a register position: a vreg in a register is already there; a
 spilled vreg or an immediate is staged into the given caller-saved scratch.
 Used for the operands of a compare, where x86 has no immediate-on-the-left
 form and only a signed-32-bit immediate on the right.

  LirOperReg = record {
   state : CodegenState,
   reg : Integer
  }

  lir-load-oper : CodegenState, LirValue, Integer, List Location -> LirOperReg
  lir-load-oper (st) (v) (scratch) (assign) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> LirOperReg { state = st, reg = list-at lir-phys pr }
      is LocSlot (ks) -> LirOperReg { state = st-append-code st (mov-load scratch reg-rsp (lir-slot-off ks)), reg = scratch }
    is LvImm (k) -> LirOperReg { state = st-append-code st (li scratch k), reg = scratch }

 A compare sets flags for the following conditional branch. The left operand
 goes to R11 when it is an immediate or spilled; the right uses the immediate
 form when it fits a signed 32-bit field, else it is staged into R8. The two
 scratch registers are distinct, so any operand combination is handled.

  lir-emit-cmp : CodegenState, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-cmp (st) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let ll = lir-load-oper st l lir-scratch-reg assign
   in when r
    is LvImm (k) ->
     if imm-fits-i32 k then st-append-code (ll.state) (cmp-ri (ll.reg) k)
     else let rr = lir-load-oper (ll.state) r lir-scratch2 assign
          in st-append-code (rr.state) (cmp-rr (ll.reg) (rr.reg))
    is LvReg (r2) ->
     let rr = lir-load-oper (ll.state) r lir-scratch2 assign
     in st-append-code (rr.state) (cmp-rr (ll.reg) (rr.reg))

 A test sets flags from a bitwise AND it does not keep, and is otherwise the
 compare's twin -- same operand staging, same two distinct scratch registers,
 same immediate form when the mask fits a signed 32-bit field. The mask a
 power-of-two remainder produces always fits; the staged form is here so that
 the instruction is total over its operands rather than only over the one
 case that reaches it today.

  lir-emit-test : CodegenState, LirValue, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-test (st) (l) (r) (assign) =
   let ll = lir-load-oper st l lir-scratch-reg assign
   in when r
    is LvImm (k) ->
     if imm-fits-i32 k then st-append-code (ll.state) (test-ri (ll.reg) k)
     else let rr = lir-load-oper (ll.state) r lir-scratch2 assign
          in st-append-code (rr.state) (test-rr (ll.reg) (rr.reg))
    is LvReg (r2) ->
     let rr = lir-load-oper (ll.state) r lir-scratch2 assign
     in st-append-code (rr.state) (test-rr (ll.reg) (rr.reg))

Section: LIR Function Emission (x86-64)

 A pending branch fixup: a rel32 slot (`patch-pos`) whose value is
 `target-block-offset - next-ip`, resolved once every block has been laid
 out and its offset recorded. Branch targets are forward or backward block
 ids, and the block offsets are not known until layout completes, so the
 branch is emitted with a zero placeholder and patched at the end.

  LirFix = record {
   patch-pos : Integer,
   next-ip : Integer,
   target : Integer
  }

 The emission context threaded through a function's blocks: the codegen
 state, the byte offset of each block indexed by its (dense) id, and the
 list of pending branch fixups.

 `nused` and `frame` ride along because a `LiRet` returns from the middle of
 the layout and has to account for them. `epi` is the block id the shared
 epilogue is laid out at -- one past the real blocks, so it can be a branch
 target like any other and be patched by the same machinery.

  LirCtx = record {
   state : CodegenState,
   boffs : List Integer,
   fixups : List LirFix,
   nused : Integer,
   frame : Integer,
   epi : Integer,
   share : Boolean,
   canon : List Integer
  }

 Whether the epilogue is worth sharing. Every `LiRet` puts its value in RAX;
 what follows -- releasing the frame, restoring the callee-saved registers,
 returning -- is the same sequence at every exit. Duplicating costs its whole
 length at each return, `nrets * e`.

 Sharing can cost less than it looks. The epilogue is laid out one past the
 last block, so if what the layout ends with is a return, that one does not
 jump -- it falls in -- and only the other `nrets - 1` pay. But the layout does
 not have to end with a return. A loop whose back-edge block is laid out last
 ends in a jump, and then every return pays and sharing costs `nrets + e`.
 Which of the two it is, is a question about the blocks, so it is asked
 (`lir-falls-into-epi`) rather than assumed.

 Both prices factor over the same product. Sharing saves `(nrets-1) * (e-1)`,
 and the fall-through is worth exactly one unit of threshold:

   falls  -> `nrets*e > (nrets-1)+e`  <=>  `(nrets-1)*(e-1) > 0`
   !falls -> `nrets*e >  nrets   +e`  <=>  `(nrets-1)*(e-1) > 1`

 So: **share when the saving beats the threshold, and the threshold is one
 when nothing falls in.** Both terms are counts and never negative.

 It lands where it should, and the answers it is easy to get wrong are the
 ones the factored form makes obvious. `sum-to` is a leaf that saved nothing,
 so `e` is `ret` alone: the product is zero, there is nothing to amortise, and
 duplicating one instruction beats jumping to it. `ack` returns from a single
 arm -- its other paths are back-edges -- so the product is zero again: one
 return has no second copy to save. `fib` has three exits and saves two
 registers, and four beats either threshold.

 Three cuts, and each one was wrong in a way the next made visible. The first
 ignored `nrets` and asked only whether the epilogue was longer than a jump: it
 bought fib three instructions and quietly charged one each to ack, locals and
 tak, every one a single-return function, and the total came out exactly where
 it started. The second charged a jump at every return including the one that
 falls through, which reads as a tie at two returns and a two-instruction
 epilogue and duplicates instead -- `regright` is that shape and it cost an
 instruction against the tree emitter. The third assumed the fall-through it
 had just started pricing, and at that same cell with a back-edge laid out last
 it shares on a tie and pays two five-byte jumps and an epilogue where two
 two-byte epilogues would do.

 **No benchmark can show that last one and none ever will.** At `(2,2)` the
 two costs are exactly equal in instructions -- `nrets*e >= nrets+e` for
 `nrets,e >= 2`, with equality only there -- so the only witness is bytes and
 executed jumps. It survived two rounds of measurement for that reason. The
 `epishare` bench is the shape; the harness reports bytes beside instructions
 because instructions cannot see it.

  lir-epi-len : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-epi-len (nused) (frame) =
   (if frame > 0 then 1 else 0) + nused + 1

 Whether the layout ends in a return: the last block that emits anything, since
 blocks that emit nothing are not where control comes to rest.

  lir-ends-in-ret : List LirInsn -> Boolean
  lir-ends-in-ret (insns) =
   let n = list-length insns
   in if n == 0 then False
      else when list-at insns (n - 1)
       is LiRet (v) -> True
       is otherwise -> False

  lir-falls-into-epi : List LirBlock, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-falls-into-epi (blocks) (i) =
   if i < 0 then False
   else if list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns) > 0 then lir-ends-in-ret ((list-at blocks i).insns)
   else lir-falls-into-epi blocks (i - 1)

  lir-epi-shared : Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Boolean
  lir-epi-shared (nrets) (nused) (frame) (falls) =
   (nrets - 1) * (lir-epi-len nused frame - 1) > (if falls then 0 else 1)

  lir-count-rets-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-count-rets-insns (insns) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiRet (v) -> lir-count-rets-insns insns (i + 1) n (acc + 1)
    is otherwise -> lir-count-rets-insns insns (i + 1) n acc

  lir-count-rets : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-count-rets (blocks) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in lir-count-rets blocks (i + 1) n (lir-count-rets-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) acc)

 A conditional branch lowers to a `jcc` to the taken block and a `jmp` to
 the fall block, but a jump to the block that is physically next in the
 layout is a fallthrough and is elided. `next-id` is the id of the block laid
 out immediately after this one (-1 for the last). Three cases: the fall
 block is next -> emit only `jcc cond -> taken` and fall into it; the taken
 block is next -> emit `jcc (inverted cond) -> fall` and fall into the taken
 block; neither is next -> emit both. `jcc` is six bytes with its rel32 at
 offset two; `jmp` is five bytes with its rel32 at offset one.

  lir-cond-invert : LirCond -> LirCond
  lir-cond-invert (c) =
   when c
    is LcEq -> LcNe
    is LcNe -> LcEq
    is LcLt -> LcGe
    is LcGe -> LcLt
    is LcGt -> LcLe
    is LcLe -> LcGt

  lir-emit-jcc-fix : LirCtx, LirCond, Integer -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-jcc-fix (cx) (c) (target) =
   let jcc-pos = (cx.state).code-len
   in let st1 = st-append-code (cx.state) (jcc (lir-cc-of c) 0)
   in LirCtx { state = st1, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = list-push (cx.fixups) (LirFix { patch-pos = jcc-pos + 2, next-ip = jcc-pos + 6, target = target }), nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }

  lir-emit-branch : LirCtx, LirCond, Integer, Integer, Integer -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-branch (cx) (c) (bt) (be) (next-id) =
   if lir-canon-at (cx.canon) be == next-id then lir-emit-jcc-fix cx c bt
   else if lir-canon-at (cx.canon) bt == next-id then lir-emit-jcc-fix cx (lir-cond-invert c) be
   else let cx1 = lir-emit-jcc-fix cx c bt
   in lir-emit-jump cx1 be (0 - 1)

  lir-emit-jump : LirCtx, Integer, Integer -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-jump (cx) (b) (next-id) =
   if lir-canon-at (cx.canon) b == next-id then cx
   else let jmp-pos = (cx.state).code-len
   in let st1 = st-append-code (cx.state) (jmp 0)
   in LirCtx { state = st1, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = list-push (cx.fixups) (LirFix { patch-pos = jmp-pos + 1, next-ip = jmp-pos + 5, target = b }), nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }

 One instruction inside a block. Value instructions (binary, move) and the
 compare only advance the state; the terminators (branch, jump) also record
 fixups. A load never reaches a selected block (the dispatch predicate
 rejects any block containing one), so its case is a defensive no-op.

  lir-ctx-st : LirCtx, CodegenState -> LirCtx
  lir-ctx-st (cx) (st) =
   LirCtx { state = st, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }

 A return from where it stands: the value into RAX, the frame released, the
 callee-saved registers restored, `ret`. It is the same sequence the single
 exit emits, and an `if` in tail position simply has one per arm instead of
 driving every arm's value into a shared vreg to hand to one at the end.

  lir-emit-return : CodegenState, LirValue, List Location, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-return (st) (v) (assign) (nused) (frame) =
   let st1 = lir-emit-result st v assign
   in let st2 = if frame > 0 then st-append-code st1 (add-ri reg-rsp frame) else st1
   in st-append-code (lir-pop-saved st2 (nused - 1)) x86-ret

  lir-emit-binsn : LirCtx, LirInsn, Integer, List Location -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-binsn (cx) (ins) (next-id) (assign) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> LirCtx { state = lir-sel-bin (cx.state) op dst l r assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> LirCtx { state = lir-sel-mov (cx.state) dst src assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> LirCtx { state = lir-emit-cmp (cx.state) l r assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> LirCtx { state = lir-emit-test (cx.state) l r assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> LirCtx { state = lir-sel-call (cx.state) dst name args assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
    is LiRet (v) ->
     if cx.share
      then lir-emit-jump (lir-ctx-st cx (lir-emit-result (cx.state) v assign)) (cx.epi) next-id
      else lir-ctx-st cx (lir-emit-return (cx.state) v assign (cx.nused) (cx.frame))
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> lir-emit-branch cx c bt be next-id
    is LiJump (b) -> lir-emit-jump cx b next-id
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> LirCtx { state = lir-sel-load (cx.state) dst base off w sg assign, boffs = cx.boffs, fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }

  lir-emit-block-insns : LirCtx, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Location -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-block-insns (cx) (insns) (i) (n) (next-id) (assign) =
   if i >= n then cx
   else lir-emit-block-insns (lir-emit-binsn cx (list-at insns i) next-id assign) insns (i + 1) n next-id assign

 A block records its start offset (by id) before its instructions. Because
 every control transfer is an explicit branch patched afterwards, the offset
 table is the only thing layout order feeds into.

  lir-emit-block : LirCtx, LirBlock, Integer, List Location -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-block (cx) (b) (next-id) (assign) =
   let cx1 = LirCtx { state = cx.state, boffs = list-set-at (cx.boffs) (b.id) ((cx.state).code-len), fixups = cx.fixups, nused = cx.nused, frame = cx.frame, epi = cx.epi, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
   in lir-emit-block-insns cx1 (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) next-id assign

 What a block falls into is the next block that emits anything, which is not
 always the next block. When every path returns, the lowering still pushes the
 exit block so that ids stay dense for the tables indexed by them, and that
 block is empty and unreachable. It occupies no bytes, so the block before it
 falls past it -- and if nothing after it emits either, what the last arm falls
 into is the epilogue at `n`. Answering with the literally-next id instead hid
 that from the jump elision, and the last returning arm jumped to the
 instruction already sitting under it.

  lir-next-emitting : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-next-emitting (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then n
   else if list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns) > 0 then (list-at blocks i).id
   else lir-next-emitting blocks (i + 1) n

 A block that emits nothing is not a place. Control never rests there: every
 byte addressed by its id belongs to whatever emits next, so naming it and
 naming its successor name the same instruction. `canon` says which id is the
 one the machine can actually see -- for each block, the first block at or
 after its layout position that emits anything, or the epilogue at `n` when
 none does. A block that emits is its own canonical form.

 Both sides of the elision must speak this language or neither does. The
 first cut of the fall-through canonicalised only what the layout falls INTO
 and left the jump's own target raw, so an arm ending `LiJump exit` compared
 the exit block's id against the epilogue's and missed: `let v = if c then a
 else b in v` seals an empty exit that both arms jump to, and each of them
 emitted `E9 00 00 00 00` -- five bytes of jump to the instruction underneath.
 Every benchmark returns from its arms, so not one of them has a non-tail join
 exit and not one of them noticed.

 Ids are dense but layout order is not id order, so this is indexed by id and
 filled by position.

  lir-canon-at : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-canon-at (canon) (b) =
   if b < 0 then b
   else if b >= list-length canon then b
   else list-at canon b

  lir-canon-fill : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-canon-fill (blocks) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-canon-fill blocks (i + 1) n (list-set-at acc ((list-at blocks i).id) (lir-next-emitting blocks i n))

  lir-canon-vec : List LirBlock, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-canon-vec (blocks) (n) =
   lir-canon-fill blocks 0 n (list-set-at (lir-int-vec 0 (n + 1) 0 []) n n)

  lir-emit-blocks : LirCtx, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Location -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-blocks (cx) (blocks) (i) (n) (assign) =
   if i >= n then cx
   else let next-id = lir-next-emitting blocks (i + 1) n
   in lir-emit-blocks (lir-emit-block cx (list-at blocks i) next-id assign) blocks (i + 1) n assign

 With every block laid out and its offset known, each fixup becomes a
 concrete rel32 written straight into the code buffer.

  lir-apply-fixups : CodegenState, List LirFix, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-apply-fixups (st) (fixups) (boffs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then st
   else let fx = list-at fixups i
   in let rel = (list-at boffs (fx.target)) - (fx.next-ip)
   in lir-apply-fixups (st-add-deferred-patch st (make-i32-patch (fx.patch-pos) rel)) fixups boffs (i + 1) n

 Save and restore the callee-saved local registers the allocation used, in
 the fixed order `RBX, R12, R13, R14`, pushed ascending and popped
 descending, and reserve/release a fixed-size frame for the spill slots. No
 frame pointer and no stack guard: a whitelisted body makes no call, so it
 cannot grow the stack, exactly the pure-leaf discipline
 `emit-function-minimal` already relies on. The frame is reserved after the
 callee-saved pushes, so a slot's `[RSP + k*8]` address is stable through the
 body (nothing in it touches RSP), and released before the pops.

 Whether the function makes any call -- if so it is not a pure leaf, and (when
 the build has the stack guard on) its prologue keeps the heap-collision
 check `cmp rsp, r10; jb __out_of_memory`, the same guard the tree emitter
 emits for non-leaves and the reason the recursion chain is caught before it
 grows the stack into the heap. The jump position is recorded in
 `stack-overflow-checks` and patched to `__out_of_memory` at finalize,
 exactly as `emit-prologue` does.

  lir-has-call-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-has-call-insns (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> True
    is otherwise -> lir-has-call-insns insns (i + 1) n

  lir-func-has-call : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-func-has-call (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else if lir-has-call-insns ((list-at blocks i).insns) 0 (list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns)) then True
   else lir-func-has-call blocks (i + 1) n

  lir-stack-guard : CodegenState -> CodegenState
  lir-stack-guard (st) =
   let st1 = st-append-code st (cmp-rr reg-rsp reg-r10)
   in let check-pos = st1.code-len
   in let st2 = st-append-code st1 (jcc cc-b 0)
   in __record-set st2 "stack-overflow-checks" (list-push (st2.stack-overflow-checks) check-pos)

 These index `lir-phys`, not `local-regs`, and the difference is the whole
 point. What must be saved is whatever physical register the allocator
 handed out, and the allocator reaches its registers through `lir-phys`:
 `LocReg (r)` means `lir-phys[r]`. Pushing `local-regs[i]` instead was
 correct only for as long as the two lists agreed on their first four
 entries, which they did, by hand, from two different files. Reorder
 either prefix and the prologue saves one register while the body uses
 another: the caller's value in the register nobody saved is gone, the
 saved register is restored over nothing, and there is no fault and no
 diagnostic to say so. The prose above already makes this argument for
 `nregs` against `lir-phys` and puts them adjacent so they are read
 together; the callee-save list is the same hazard and was split across
 files instead. Deriving costs nothing and cannot drift.

  lir-push-saved : CodegenState, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-push-saved (st) (i) (nused) =
   if i >= nused then st
   else lir-push-saved (st-append-code st (push-r (list-at lir-phys i))) (i + 1) nused

  lir-pop-saved : CodegenState, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-pop-saved (st) (i) =
   if i < 0 then st
   else lir-pop-saved (st-append-code st (pop-r (list-at lir-phys i))) (i - 1)

 Move each parameter from the register it arrives in (`arg-regs[i]`) to the
 location the allocator gave its vreg -- a register, or a store into its spill
 slot. At most six parameters; the dispatch predicate refuses a seventh, which
 would arrive on the caller's stack.

 **This is a parallel move, and the reason is a lesson.** It used to be a
 sequence, one move per parameter in parameter order, on the stated grounds
 that every destination was a callee-saved register or a stack slot and so
 disjoint from the argument registers. That was true the day it was written.
 Then the leaf register file put `RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX` into `lir-phys` at
 `5..8`, so that a leaf could keep its parameters in the registers they
 arrived in -- and from that moment a destination could be *another
 parameter's arrival register*. The comment kept vouching for an invariant the
 code had lost, and nothing re-checked it.

 What it cost: `col-hue` in the foreword's Color chapter takes five parameters
 and divides. The division masks RDX out of the free set for every value live
 across it, so parameter 2 cannot keep the register it arrived in and is
 placed in RCX -- which is where parameter 3 arrives. In order, that emits
 `mov rcx,rdx` and then `mov rbx,rcx`, and the second move reads a register
 the first one has already overwritten: `mx` is handed `b`. `col-hue` answers
 60 where it should answer 0, `rgb-to-hsl` reports red as yellow, and the
 whole thing was invisible to every gate the campaign had -- the benchmarks
 measure speed and this is an answer, the fixed point proves determinism and a
 deterministic wrong answer is still a fixed point, and the allocation
 verifier never sees these moves at all because they are emitted before the
 blocks and are not LIR instructions.

 So: emit a parameter's move only when no parameter still to be moved reads
 the register it writes. When every remaining move is blocked, the moves form
 a cycle; stage one source through R11 (never allocated, and not an argument
 register) and the cycle opens. A destination that is a spill slot blocks
 nothing -- it is memory, and clobbers no register. This is exactly what
 `lir-place-args` does for call arguments twenty lines up; the same problem
 deserved the same answer the first time.

  lir-pmv-srcs : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-pmv-srcs (i) (np) (acc) =
   if i >= np then acc
   else lir-pmv-srcs (i + 1) np (list-push acc (list-at arg-regs i))

  lir-pmv-dst : List Location, Integer -> Integer
  lir-pmv-dst (assign) (i) =
   when (list-at assign i)
    is LocReg (pr) -> list-at lir-phys pr
    is LocSlot (ks) -> 0 - 1

  lir-pmv-blocked : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-pmv-blocked (srcs) (done) (i) (np) (dsti) (j) =
   if j >= np then False
   else if j == i then lir-pmv-blocked srcs done i np dsti (j + 1)
   else if list-at done j then lir-pmv-blocked srcs done i np dsti (j + 1)
   else if (list-at srcs j) == dsti then True
   else lir-pmv-blocked srcs done i np dsti (j + 1)

  lir-pmv-pick : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, List Location -> Integer
  lir-pmv-pick (srcs) (done) (i) (np) (assign) =
   if i >= np then 0 - 1
   else if list-at done i then lir-pmv-pick srcs done (i + 1) np assign
   else let d = lir-pmv-dst assign i
   in if d < 0 then i
      else if lir-pmv-blocked srcs done i np d 0 then lir-pmv-pick srcs done (i + 1) np assign
      else i

  lir-pmv-stageable : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-pmv-stageable (srcs) (done) (i) (np) =
   if i >= np then 0 - 1
   else if list-at done i then lir-pmv-stageable srcs done (i + 1) np
   else if (list-at srcs i) == lir-scratch-reg then lir-pmv-stageable srcs done (i + 1) np
   else i

 The move order is decided first and emitted second, as a plan rather than as
 bytes. A plan can be read back, and that is the whole point: the parallel
 move above is checked below by simulating it, and a checker that re-ran the
 planner's own reasoning would agree with it however wrong it was.

  PmvStep =
   | PmvReg (Integer) (Integer)
   | PmvStore (Integer) (Integer)

  lir-pmv-step : List Location, Integer, Integer -> PmvStep
  lir-pmv-step (assign) (i) (srcreg) =
   when (list-at assign i)
    is LocReg (pr) -> PmvReg (list-at lir-phys pr) srcreg
    is LocSlot (ks) -> PmvStore ks srcreg

 `lir-pmv-stage` exists to read `srcs[c]` before the same call overwrites it.
 `list-set-at` writes in place, and arguments evaluate left to right, so a
 staging step built in the argument list after the rewrite would read R11 back
 and stage a register onto itself.

  lir-pmv-stage : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, List Location, Integer, List PmvStep, Integer, Integer -> List PmvStep
  lir-pmv-stage (srcs) (done) (np) (assign) (fuel) (acc) (c) (sc) =
   lir-pmv-plan (list-set-at srcs c lir-scratch-reg) done np assign fuel (list-push acc (PmvReg lir-scratch-reg sc))

  lir-pmv-plan : List Integer, List Boolean, Integer, List Location, Integer, List PmvStep -> List PmvStep
  lir-pmv-plan (srcs) (done) (np) (assign) (fuel) (acc) =
   if fuel <= 0 then acc
   else let p = lir-pmv-pick srcs done 0 np assign
   in if p >= 0
      then lir-pmv-plan srcs (list-set-at done p True) np assign (fuel - 1) (list-push acc (lir-pmv-step assign p (list-at srcs p)))
      else let c = lir-pmv-stageable srcs done 0 np
      in if c < 0 then acc
         else lir-pmv-stage srcs done np assign (fuel - 1) acc c (list-at srcs c)

  lir-pmv-emit : CodegenState, List PmvStep, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-pmv-emit (st) (plan) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then st
   else let step = list-at plan i
   in when step
    is PmvReg (d) (s) -> lir-pmv-emit (lir-move-param-reg st d s) plan (i + 1) n
    is PmvStore (k) (s) -> lir-pmv-emit (st-append-code st (mov-store reg-rsp s (lir-slot-off k))) plan (i + 1) n

 The prologue check. The allocation verifier in `IR/Lir.codex` walks the LIR
 and asserts that every use reads its vreg from the location the assignment
 gave it -- but it begins by *seeding* the parameters into their assigned
 locations, which is to say it assumes the entry moves worked. That assumption
 is the one thing here that was never true, and `[alloc-check: ok]` printed on
 `col-hue` for ten sessions while `col-hue` was miscompiled.

 So this checks the assumption instead of inheriting it. Seed a register file
 with each parameter in the register it really arrives in, apply the planned
 moves to that file one at a time, and then ask whether every parameter ended
 up where the assignment promised. A move that clobbers a register a later move
 still reads shows up as the wrong parameter index sitting in the destination.
 The file is indexed by physical register (0..15) with spill slot `k` at
 `16 + k`, because these moves are physical -- they are what establishes the
 vreg-to-location invariant that the abstract LIR takes as given.

 It halts rather than warns. A warning here is a warning that the binary about
 to be written computes something other than the program, which is not a thing
 a build should survive; `IR/IRCheck.codex` reaches the same conclusion about
 malformed IR for the same reason.

  pmv-nphys : Integer = 16

  pmv-loc-sim : Location -> Integer
  pmv-loc-sim (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (pr) -> list-at lir-phys pr
    is LocSlot (k) -> pmv-nphys + k

  pmv-seed : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  pmv-seed (file) (i) (np) =
   if i >= np then file
   else pmv-seed (list-set-at file (list-at arg-regs i) i) (i + 1) np

  pmv-apply : List Integer, List PmvStep, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  pmv-apply (file) (plan) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then file
   else let step = list-at plan i
   in when step
    is PmvReg (d) (s) -> pmv-apply (list-set-at file d (list-at file s)) plan (i + 1) n
    is PmvStore (k) (s) -> pmv-apply (list-set-at file (pmv-nphys + k) (list-at file s)) plan (i + 1) n

  pmv-viol : List Integer, List Location, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pmv-viol (file) (assign) (i) (np) =
   if i >= np then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at file (pmv-loc-sim (list-at assign i))) == i then pmv-viol file assign (i + 1) np
   else i

  lir-move-params : CodegenState, Text, Integer, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-move-params (st) (fname) (np) (assign) =
   let plan = lir-pmv-plan (lir-pmv-srcs 0 np []) (lir-make-vec 0 np []) np assign (np * 2 + 4) []
   in let nsim = pmv-nphys + lir-assign-max-slot assign 0 (list-length assign) 0
   in let file = pmv-apply (pmv-seed (lir-int-vec 0 nsim (0 - 1) []) 0 np) plan 0 (list-length plan)
   in let bad = pmv-viol file assign 0 np
   in if bad < 0 then lir-pmv-emit st plan 0 (list-length plan)
      else st-add-error st cdx-lir-prologue-violation ("The entry moves for '" & fname & "' do not place parameter " & show bad & " in " & lir-loc-text (list-at assign bad) & ", the location its vreg was allocated. Simulating the planned moves from the argument registers leaves some other parameter's value there, so this function would run with parameter " & show bad & " holding a value that is not its own. The fault is in the selector's parallel move, not in the program being compiled: a move was emitted that overwrote a register a later move still had to read.") synthetic-span

 A parameter whose register is the one it arrived in needs no instruction.
 This is the common case in a leaf: the leaf register file lists the argument
 registers in argument order, and the parameters are allocated first against a
 free file, so parameter `i` is usually handed exactly `arg-regs[i]` and the
 entry move is `mov rdi, rdi`. Usually, not always -- see above.

  lir-move-param-reg : CodegenState, Integer, Integer -> CodegenState
  lir-move-param-reg (st) (dst) (src) =
   if dst == src then st
   else st-append-code st (mov-rr dst src)

 The function result goes into RAX, the return register, before the frame is
 released and the callee-saved registers restored (so the value is copied out
 while its register or slot still holds it). The result is defined in the
 exit block, which the lowering appends last, so the result move and epilogue
 physically follow the exit block's code and a branch into the exit falls
 through into them.

  lir-emit-result : CodegenState, LirValue, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-result (st) (res) (assign) =
   when res
    is LvImm (k) -> st-append-code st (li reg-rax k)
    is LvReg (r) ->
     when (list-at assign r)
      is LocReg (pr) -> lir-move-param-reg st reg-rax (list-at lir-phys pr)
      is LocSlot (ks) -> st-append-code st (mov-load reg-rax reg-rsp (lir-slot-off ks))

 The whole function: record its offset and reset per-function state (the
 same `reset-func-state` the tree path uses, so the offset lands in the
 shared capacity-backed `fo-names`/`fo-offsets` buffers and calls resolve to
 it), then prologue, frame reservation, parameter moves, the blocks (with
 branch fixups applied once laid out), the result, frame release, epilogue,
 return. A straight-line body with no spill is the degenerate case: one
 block, no terminator, no fixups, no frame.

  lir-emit-func : CodegenState, LirFunc, List Location -> CodegenState
  lir-emit-func (st) (f) (assign) =
   let st0 = reset-func-state st (f.name)
   in let nused = lir-nused-callee assign 0 (list-length assign) 0
   in let frame = lir-nslots assign 0 (list-length assign) 0 * 8
   in let has-call = lir-func-has-call (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks))
   in let st1 = lir-push-saved st0 0 nused
   in let stg = if has-call & (st0.stack-guard) then lir-stack-guard st1 else st1
   in let st1b = if frame > 0 then st-append-code stg (emit-sub-rsp-imm32 frame) else stg
   in let st2 = lir-move-params st1b (f.name) (f.nparams) assign
   in let nb = list-length (f.blocks)
   in let nrets = lir-count-rets (f.blocks) 0 nb 0
   in let cx0 = LirCtx { state = st2, boffs = lir-int-vec 0 (nb + 1) 0 [], fixups = [], nused = nused, frame = frame, epi = nb, share = lir-epi-shared nrets nused frame (lir-falls-into-epi (f.blocks) (nb - 1)), canon = lir-canon-vec (f.blocks) nb }
   in let cxf = lir-emit-blocks cx0 (f.blocks) 0 nb assign
   in let cxe = lir-emit-epilogue cxf f assign nb nused frame
   in lir-apply-fixups (cxe.state) (cxe.fixups) (cxe.boffs) 0 (list-length (cxe.fixups))

 The epilogue is laid out after every block, at the id the returns branch to.
 A function with a single exit falls into it with its result operand, exactly
 as before. A function whose every path returns emits it once if it is worth
 sharing, and not at all if each return already carried its own.

  lir-emit-epilogue : LirCtx, LirFunc, List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> LirCtx
  lir-emit-epilogue (cx) (f) (assign) (nb) (nused) (frame) =
   let cx1 = LirCtx { state = cx.state, boffs = list-set-at (cx.boffs) nb ((cx.state).code-len), fixups = cx.fixups, nused = nused, frame = frame, epi = nb, share = cx.share, canon = cx.canon }
   in if f.rets == False
      then lir-ctx-st cx1 (lir-emit-return (cx1.state) (f.result) assign nused frame)
      else if cx.share
      then lir-ctx-st cx1 (st-append-code (lir-pop-saved (if frame > 0 then st-append-code (cx1.state) (add-ri reg-rsp frame) else (cx1.state)) (nused - 1)) x86-ret)
      else cx1

Section: LIR Dispatch

 The dispatch predicate -- the ratchet. A definition is selected for the LIR
 path only when every gate holds:

  - it is not `punctual` (the LIR path does not yet emit the WCET-checked
    prologue, so a punctual function stays on the tree path that does);
  - it has at most six parameters (a seventh arrives on the caller's stack,
    which the parameter mover does not read);
  - no parameter and no return type carries a narrowing `OvError` bound (the
    tree path inserts Eiffel-style range traps for those; the LIR path does
    not emit them yet, so a bounded signature falls back rather than losing
    its check);
  - and the body lowers to the LIR whitelist at all (`lower-def-to-lir`),
    which is the only remaining gate now that the selector handles every
    instruction the lowering can emit.

 Any failing gate answers "not handled" and the caller keeps the tree
 emitter. The predicate re-runs the lowering and allocation the emission then
 consumes; both are cheap, deterministic, and reclaimed with the rest of the
 per-function scratch by the `__heap-save`/`__heap-restore` bracket
 `emit-all-defs` wraps around every function.

  lir-ty-needs-guard : CodexType -> Boolean
  lir-ty-needs-guard (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) ->
     when mode
      is OvError -> (lo > i64-min) | (hi < i64-max)
      is otherwise -> False
    is otherwise -> False

  lir-params-plain : List IRParam, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-params-plain (ps) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if lir-ty-needs-guard ((list-at ps i).type-val) then False
   else lir-params-plain ps (i + 1) n

 A call target is acceptable only when it is a plain user function called at
 its exact arity and it is not a builtin. This is the gate that keeps
 constructors, effect operations, over- and partial applications, and
 indirect calls on the tree path: a constructor or effect-op name is not in
 the user-arity table (so the arity lookup fails), an over/partial call has a
 mismatched arity, and an indirect call left the lowering with an empty root
 name and never became a `LiCall`. The arity and builtin tables live on the
 codegen state, which is why this gate runs in the selector, not in the
 target-independent lowering.

  lir-call-target-ok : CodegenState, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-call-target-ok (st) (name) (nargs) =
   (lookup-x86-arity (st.user-arities) name == nargs) & (is-builtin (st.builtin-names) name == False)

  lir-calls-ok-insns : CodegenState, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-calls-ok-insns (st) (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> if lir-call-target-ok st name (list-length args) then lir-calls-ok-insns st insns (i + 1) n else False
    is otherwise -> lir-calls-ok-insns st insns (i + 1) n

  lir-calls-ok : CodegenState, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-calls-ok (st) (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if lir-calls-ok-insns st ((list-at blocks i).insns) 0 (list-length ((list-at blocks i).insns))
        then lir-calls-ok st blocks (i + 1) n
        else False

  LirEmitTry = record {
   handled : Boolean,
   state : CodegenState
  }

  lir-not-handled : CodegenState -> LirEmitTry
  lir-not-handled (st) = LirEmitTry { handled = False, state = st }

 The backstop for the loop-spanning intervals. `lir-build-intervals` extends
 every parameter across the whole body on the argument that in a self-tail-
 recursive function the parameters are the only loop-carried values. This
 checks that claim on the lowered function instead of trusting it: nothing
 but a parameter may be live into the header. A function that carried some
 other value around the loop would need real loop-aware interval
 construction, so it is declined and the tree emitter keeps it. For clean
 self-tail-recursion the check always passes; it exists so that the day it
 does not, the result is a fallback and not a miscompile.

  lir-header-live-ok : List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-header-live-ok (live) (i) (nv) (np) =
   if i >= nv then True
   else if (list-at live i) & (i >= np) then False
   else lir-header-live-ok live (i + 1) nv np

  lir-loop-shape-ok : LirFunc -> Boolean
  lir-loop-shape-ok (f) =
   if lir-has-backedge f == False then True
   else let livein = lir-liveness f
   in if list-length livein == 0 then False
      else lir-header-live-ok (list-at livein 0) 0 (f.nvregs) (f.nparams)

 The verifiers stand on the emission path. Both were written as dump-only
 diagnostics, and dump-only is what let a coalescing bug ship wrong bytes
 while `lir-check-func` would have named the exact violation: nothing ran
 it. So every function the selector emits is checked first -- the lowered
 LIR structurally, then the actual assignment the emitter is about to use
 (not a re-derived one) -- and a violation halts the build the way the
 prologue check does. The cost is a walk over blocks the emitter walks
 anyway, on per-function scratch the emit loop already reclaims.

  lir-verifier-halt : CodegenState, Integer between 0 and 65535, Text, Text, List Text -> CodegenState
  lir-verifier-halt (st) (code) (which) (fname) (viol) =
   st-add-error st code ("The " & which & " verifier rejected '" & fname & "' on the emission path: " & (list-at viol 0) & (if list-length viol > 1 then " (and " & show (list-length viol - 1) & " more)" else "") & ". The selector's output would not compute the program; the build halts rather than write it.") synthetic-span

  lir-emit-try : CodegenState, IRDef -> LirEmitTry
  lir-emit-try (st) (def) =
   if def.is-punctual then lir-not-handled st
   else if list-length (def.params) > 6 then lir-not-handled st
   else if lir-params-plain (def.params) 0 (list-length (def.params)) == False then lir-not-handled st
   else if lir-ty-needs-guard (return-bound-of (def.type-val) (list-length (def.params))) then lir-not-handled st
   else let r = lower-def-to-lir def
   in if r.ok == False then lir-not-handled st
      else if lir-calls-ok st ((r.func).blocks) 0 (list-length ((r.func).blocks)) == False then lir-not-handled st
         else if lir-loop-shape-ok (r.func) == False then lir-not-handled st
         else let sviol = lir-check-func (r.func)
         in if list-length sviol > 0 then LirEmitTry { handled = True, state = lir-verifier-halt st cdx-lir-structural-violation "structural" (def.name) sviol }
            else let a = lir-alloc-linscan lir-target-x86 (r.func)
            in let aviol = lir-alloc-check lir-target-x86 (r.func) (a.assign)
            in if list-length aviol > 0 then LirEmitTry { handled = True, state = lir-verifier-halt st cdx-lir-allocation-violation "allocation" (def.name) aviol }
               else LirEmitTry { handled = True, state = lir-emit-func st (r.func) (a.assign) }

Page 14 of 14
